  "worked_solution": "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $13,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{13}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $7,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{7}.$\n\nIn $P+Q,$ the highest-order term still contains an ${x}^{13}.$ No ${x}^{13}$ term in $Q$ could possibly cancel out the ${x}^{13}$ term in $P.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P+Q$ must be $13.$",
